FOLLOW-UP, measured on a GitHub hosted runner 2026-07-31.

Wiring this gate into linux-containers made it run on ubuntu-latest for the
first time, and it found an environment limit the local runs could not:

  PASS captured a long running failure into a process capsule
  PASS replay from zero reproduces the failure with the config deleted
  Error: criu could not checkpoint the replaying process ... neither can one
  whose files live on a mount criu cannot resolve
  FAIL could not anchor the replaying process

criu 3.17.1 installs and the capsule captures and replays, but criu cannot
resolve the mounts of a container nested inside a hosted runner, so DUMP
fails. The same shape was hit independently while fixing the deferred
capsule defects (Can't lookup mount=30 for fd=0 path=/dev/null).

This left three options, and the first two are both bad:
  1. leave the gate unwired, which is how the criu 4.1.1 drift survived;
  2. wire it fully and accept a permanently red job, which trains everyone
     to ignore the workflow;
  3. run the half CI can prove and say plainly which half it did not.

Option 3: REPROIT_CHECKPOINT_SCOPE=replay-only stops after case 2, asserts
exactly 2 cases ran, and prints NOT EVALUATED for anchoring, restore and the
three refusals. It is registered as a SEPARATE gate id, process-capsule-longrun,
so a green subset can never be read as a green process-checkpoint. The full
gate stays 7/7 and is marked manual, to be run where criu can dump.

CORRECTION, same day. Marking the full gate 'manual' with prose in its
automation.job field broke a registry test:

  process-checkpoint workflow does not define job run by hand where criu
  can dump; see MEASUREMENT.md
  crates/reproit/src/adapters/platform.rs:504

every_registered_platform_has_a_runtime_evidence_gate resolves the named job
against the named workflow, so the job field is not a free-text slot. The
registry already had the right shape: mode 'manual-required' plus a non-empty
'blocker' string, which that same test asserts is present, precisely so a gate
cannot be parked without stating what stops it.

After the correction: 993 lib tests pass, and the specific test is confirmed
to have RUN rather than been filtered out:

  test adapters::platform::tests::every_registered_platform_has_a_runtime_evidence_gate ... ok
  test result: ok. 993 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 filtered out

Worth recording that the first check of this fix ran ZERO tests ("0 passed;
0 failed; 1 filtered out") because the filter matched nothing in the binary
under test, and an empty run reports ok. That is the same shape as every
other defect this file exists for, met while fixing one of them.
